---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Portrait from "../components/Portrait.astro";
import Invite from "../components/Invite.astro";
import { pageTitle } from "../consts";

const description =
	"Akai Milson teaches 11th Grade English and AP Seminar at Olympia High School and designs curriculum that helps students think clearly, research honestly, and write in their own voice.";
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle("About")} description={description}>
	<section class="about-intro">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div class="stack">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1>An English classroom built for thinking</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					I teach 11th Grade English and AP Seminar at Olympia High School. I design curriculum,
					and I pay close attention to the tools students already have in their pockets.
				</p>
				<div class="prose">
					<p>
						Both courses ask young people to do something difficult in public: decide what they
						think, show how they got there, and stay open to being wrong. My job is to make that
						practice daily, specific, and possible for the students in front of me.
					</p>
					<p>
						Administrators, parents, colleagues, and conference organizers work with me when they
						want a classroom culture that treats inquiry as the main event. The materials on this
						site are the same ones I put in students’ hands.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<Portrait size="aside" />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="mission-heading">
		<div class="wrap measure prose">
			<h2 id="mission-heading">The mission</h2>
			<p>
				Students should leave able to ask a sharper question than the one they walked in with, and
				able to explain their reasoning to someone who does not already agree. Critical thinking,
				in this classroom, is a set of moves: notice a complication, find out who has already
				studied it, weigh perspectives, and build a line of reasoning a reader can follow.
			</p>
			<p>
				Clarity is the other half. A sophisticated idea that cannot be said plainly is not finished.
				We revise for logic and for the sentence. Voice stays. Fog goes.
			</p>
			<p>
				That mission is the same when I am designing a unit, conferring on a draft, or deciding
				whether a new tool belongs in the room. If it does not help a student think or communicate
				more clearly, it does not stay.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section band-warm" aria-labelledby="design-heading">
		<div class="wrap split-even">
			<article class="panel">
				<p class="kicker">Curriculum design</p>
				<h2 id="design-heading">Backward from the thinking</h2>
				<p>
					I design units backward from the intellectual work students will do in public. A literary
					essay, a seminar discussion, an Individual Research Report, and a team presentation are
					different products. They practice related muscles: questioning, reading across
					perspectives, using evidence, and shaping an argument for a real audience.
				</p>
				<p>
					Organizers earn a place only when they teach a move a student can later do without them.
					Early in a task, the scaffold is visible. Later, it comes off. The goal is independence,
					not prettier worksheets.
				</p>
				<p>
					When I collaborate with a department or an administrator, we start from those moves and
					from the students we actually teach. The map has to be usable on a Tuesday.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article class="panel">
				<p class="kicker">Educational technology</p>
				<h2>Tools in service of judgment</h2>
				<p>
					I am a technology enthusiast in a specific way. I try new tools, including generative AI,
					and I test them against one question: does this make student thinking more visible, or
					does it hide the thinking inside a fluent answer?
				</p>
				<p>
					A shared document can show a team where an argument is thin. A recording lets a student
					hear the moment they lost the audience. A search tool can widen a source list. None of
					those tools get to own the claim.
				</p>
				<p>
					Families and colleagues often want a practical policy, not a slogan. The
					<a href="/resources#ai-tech">AI and technology resources</a> are the classroom version:
					what is allowed, what must be disclosed, and what still has to be the student’s own reasoning.
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="collaborate-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<div class="section-head">
				<p class="eyebrow">Collaboration</p>
				<h2 id="collaborate-heading">Why people write</h2>
				<p class="lede">
					The work travels because it is concrete. Here is what each conversation is usually for.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="audience-grid">
				<article class="audience-card">
					<h3>Administrators</h3>
					<p>
						Instructional walks, curriculum alignment between English and AP Seminar, and
						professional learning that teachers can use the same week. I can talk through task
						design, academic integrity, and how AI policy shows up in actual assignments.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="audience-card">
					<h3>Parents and guardians</h3>
					<p>
						Questions about research tasks, writing feedback, deadlines, and how to support a
						student without writing the paper for them. I would rather explain the reasoning behind
						a task than leave a family guessing.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="audience-card">
					<h3>Colleagues</h3>
					<p>
						Shared protocols, resource swaps, and classroom visits. If a graphic organizer or a
						fact-checking routine is useful in your room, take it. If you have a better version,
						I want to see it.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article class="audience-card">
					<h3>Conference organizers</h3>
					<p>
						Workshops on student fact-checking, AI-assisted research that keeps critical thinking
						intact, and scaffolding academic writing. Sessions are built around a protocol
						participants can rehearse, not a slide deck they will forget.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<Invite />
</BaseLayout>
